<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <!--
    The warning gate. It sits in this file for the OPPOSITE reason the bans below do: the late import is
    what stops a project overriding it. Stated in Directory.Build.props, a project could set
    TreatWarningsAsErrors=false and compile with warnings while every other project was held to zero —
    invisible in a green build, and exactly the kind of exception nobody goes back to re-examine. Nothing
    overrides it today; this is what keeps that from resting on trust.

    Tolerating a SPECIFIC warning is still possible, and is the reviewable form of the same escape hatch:
    NoWarn, one code at a time, in the project that needs it and naming what it accepts. ihcclient is the
    only one, and it APPENDS to NoWarn rather than assigning it — assigning drops the SDK's own entries.

    Note the limit, because a target further down this file depends on it: this is handed to the COMPILER,
    so it does not promote MSBuild's own warnings to errors.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Repo-wide compile-time bans. Imported automatically into every project *after* its own body, which is
    what lets a project opt out with a property it sets itself — Directory.Build.props is imported before
    the project body and could not see such a property, so this belongs here rather than there.

    The generated SOAP layer must not be referenced outside the SDK. The architecture tests already enforce
    that for the two assemblies they load (ihcclient's own tiers and ihc_openvisual); this covers the rest
    of the repository, and reports at the edit instead of in a later test run.

    A project that legitimately uses the layer sets <UsesGeneratedSoapLayer>true</UsesGeneratedSoapLayer>
    in its own file, so each exception is visible where it is taken rather than in a list here that nothing
    would keep honest. There are three: the SDK that owns the layer, and the two suites that white-box it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UsesGeneratedSoapLayer)' != 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)BannedSymbols.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Stage the repository's ihcsettings.json beside a runnable output, and take it away again on Clean.
    This pair was copy-pasted verbatim into ten project files — every application, every utility that
    reads settings, both examples and the two suites that need credentials — so the logic is stated here
    and each project says only whether it wants it, the same shape as UsesGeneratedSoapLayer above.

    Opt IN rather than out: most projects here are libraries or controller-free suites that must not
    acquire a settings file just by existing, and a project that never reads settings getting one is the
    kind of thing nothing would notice.

    The file is optional at build time on purpose. It is gitignored (it holds a controller password), so a
    fresh clone and every CI runner build without it; the Condition is what keeps that a normal build
    rather than a missing-file error. Sourced from $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory) — the repository root by
    definition — instead of the '..\..\' each copy used, which was correct only for a project exactly two
    directories deep.
  -->
  <Target Name="CopyIhcSettingsToOutput" AfterTargets="AfterBuild"
          Condition="'$(CopyIhcSettingsToOutput)' == 'true'">
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)ihcsettings.json" DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)"
          Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)ihcsettings.json')" />
  </Target>

  <Target Name="DeleteIhcSettingsFromOutput" AfterTargets="Clean"
          Condition="'$(CopyIhcSettingsToOutput)' == 'true'">
    <Delete Files="$(OutDir)ihcsettings.json" Condition="Exists('$(OutDir)ihcsettings.json')" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    Code coverage, on by default for every test project.

    This lives in .targets rather than .props because it depends on IsTestProject, which is set by
    Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk's own props — Directory.Build.props is evaluated before those and could not
    read it. Both properties below are consumed by the VSTest task rather than at evaluation time, so
    setting them this late is still in time. Non-test projects are left untouched.

    Each suite writes its own slice. The repo's CI issues a separate `dotnet test` per suite, so a single
    shared results directory would leave the merged report describing only whichever suite ran last.
    The cost of per-suite slices is stated in the summary the merge prints: running one suite refreshes
    one slice and leaves the others as they were, so a repo-wide roll-up can carry stale slices. The
    per-suite figure is always current.

    Opt out of instrumentation for a run with `-p:CollectCoverage=false`. That property exists because
    the obvious alternative does not work: passing an empty settings file makes the `dotnet test` CLI
    itself fail with "The path is empty", before any of this is consulted. Naming a *different* settings
    file does work — the SDK prefers an explicit one over RunSettingsFilePath.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' == 'true' AND '$(CollectCoverage)' != 'false'">
    <RunSettingsFilePath Condition="'$(RunSettingsFilePath)' == ''">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).runsettings</RunSettingsFilePath>
    <CoverageRawDirectory>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)artifacts/coverage/raw/$(MSBuildProjectName)</CoverageRawDirectory>
    <CoverageReportDirectory>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)artifacts/coverage/report</CoverageReportDirectory>
    <VSTestResultsDirectory Condition="'$(VSTestResultsDirectory)' == ''">$(CoverageRawDirectory)</VSTestResultsDirectory>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Drop only THIS suite's slice. The reference implementation this was modelled on wipes the whole
    results tree before every run, which is correct for a repo that runs `dotnet test` once — here it
    would leave the merged report describing a single suite.
  -->
  <Target Name="CleanCoverageSlice"
          BeforeTargets="VSTest"
          Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' == 'true' AND '$(CollectCoverage)' != 'false'">
    <RemoveDir Directories="$(CoverageRawDirectory)" ContinueOnError="true" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    Fold every slice present into one report. Runs after each suite because the merged file is valid
    after any of them, and no single suite is reliably last: CI gates suites per OS, so the lab and
    visual suites do not run everywhere.

    Nothing here may redden a green test run (coverage is a report, not a gate), hence ContinueOnError
    throughout — a missing tool or an empty results directory warns and leaves the verdict alone.

    The tool is invoked as `dotnet dotnet-coverage`, never bare: it is a LOCAL tool, and a bare call
    resolves only if it also happens to be installed globally, which a fresh clone will not have.
  -->
  <Target Name="MergeCoverageReport"
          AfterTargets="VSTest"
          Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' == 'true' AND '$(CollectCoverage)' != 'false'">

    <ItemGroup>
      <!--
        Blame stages a byte-identical second copy of each attachment under In/<machine>/. Merging is a
        union so the percentage is unaffected either way, but merging the same bytes twice is waste.
        The exclusion names only that known staging path, so an unfamiliar layout still gets picked up
        by the wildcard rather than silently dropped.
      -->
      <CoverageSliceFile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)artifacts/coverage/raw/**/*.coverage"
                         Exclude="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)artifacts/coverage/raw/**/In/**/*.coverage" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <PropertyGroup>
      <CoverageSliceArguments>@(CoverageSliceFile->'&quot;%(FullPath)&quot;', ' ')</CoverageSliceArguments>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <MakeDir Directories="$(CoverageReportDirectory)"
             Condition="'@(CoverageSliceFile)' != ''" ContinueOnError="true" />

    <Exec Condition="'@(CoverageSliceFile)' != ''"
          WorkingDirectory="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)"
          Command="dotnet dotnet-coverage merge $(CoverageSliceArguments) -o &quot;artifacts/coverage/report/merged.coverage&quot; --nologo"
          ContinueOnError="true" StandardOutputImportance="low" />

    <Exec Condition="'@(CoverageSliceFile)' != ''"
          WorkingDirectory="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)"
          Command="dotnet dotnet-coverage merge &quot;artifacts/coverage/report/merged.coverage&quot; -o &quot;artifacts/coverage/report/coverage.xml&quot; -f xml --nologo"
          ContinueOnError="true" StandardOutputImportance="low" />

    <CallTarget Targets="GenerateCoverageHtml" ContinueOnError="true"
                Condition="'@(CoverageSliceFile)' != ''" />

    <CallTarget Targets="DisplayCoverageSummary" ContinueOnError="true"
                Condition="'@(CoverageSliceFile)' != ''" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    Browsable form of the same report, for reading a specific file's uncovered lines rather than a total.

    Fed the standard Microsoft coverage XML directly — the same file Summary.txt is built from, with no
    intermediate format. That matters because ReportGenerator derives its own coverable-line denominator
    from whatever it is given, so each conversion produces a slightly different headline percentage.
    Reading the same file keeps the two as close as the tools allow; they still do not agree exactly,
    and Summary.txt is the number this repository quotes. Converting to a third-party format first was
    measured and drifts further, so do not add a conversion step here.

    The default is the one-page summary. The full per-file drill-down is opt-in with
    `-p:CoverageHtmlDetail=true`, because it writes hundreds of files and a great deal more of them
    than anyone wants produced by every `dotnet test`.
  -->
  <Target Name="GenerateCoverageHtml"
          Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' == 'true' AND '$(CollectCoverage)' != 'false'">

    <PropertyGroup>
      <CoverageHtmlReportTypes Condition="'$(CoverageHtmlDetail)' == 'true'">Html</CoverageHtmlReportTypes>
      <CoverageHtmlReportTypes Condition="'$(CoverageHtmlReportTypes)' == ''">HtmlSummary</CoverageHtmlReportTypes>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Switching between the two modes otherwise leaves the previous mode's pages behind. -->
    <RemoveDir Directories="$(CoverageReportDirectory)/html" ContinueOnError="true" />

    <Exec Condition="Exists('$(CoverageReportDirectory)/coverage.xml')"
          WorkingDirectory="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)"
          Command="dotnet reportgenerator -reports:&quot;artifacts/coverage/report/coverage.xml&quot; -targetdir:&quot;artifacts/coverage/report/html&quot; -reporttypes:$(CoverageHtmlReportTypes) -verbosity:Warning"
          ContinueOnError="true" StandardOutputImportance="low" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    One line on the console, after the merge. Deliberately not gated on a project name: with suites
    gated per OS there is no project that is reliably last, so every run prints the current roll-up.
    Re-emitted through Message so it survives at the minimal verbosity CI runs at.
  -->
  <Target Name="DisplayCoverageSummary"
          Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' == 'true' AND '$(CollectCoverage)' != 'false'">
    <Exec WorkingDirectory="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)"
          Command="dotnet run .build/coverage-summary.cs -- &quot;artifacts/coverage/report/coverage.xml&quot; &quot;artifacts/coverage/raw&quot; &quot;artifacts/coverage/report/Summary.txt&quot;"
          ContinueOnError="true" ConsoleToMSBuild="true" StandardOutputImportance="low">
      <Output TaskParameter="ConsoleOutput" PropertyName="CoverageSummaryOutput" />
    </Exec>
    <Message Importance="high" Text="$(CoverageSummaryOutput)" Condition="'$(CoverageSummaryOutput)' != ''" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    The repository's static checks, on for every build. Opt a run out with `-p:RunStaticChecks=false`,
    the same shape as CollectCoverage above.

    WHICH checks run is deliberately absent from this file. scripts/static_check.sh and its PowerShell
    peer own that list, so a check can be added, changed or dropped by editing a script rather than the
    file every project in the repository imports — and so that the checks a build runs are exactly the
    checks a developer can run by hand. This target picks a peer by platform and translates its outcome
    into MSBuild's vocabulary; it knows nothing else about them, and naming one here would start a
    second list to keep honest.

    The one thing it does rely on is the exit code: non-zero means a check could not RUN, never that a
    check found something. These checks advise rather than gate, and their tools are not all restored by
    the build, so IgnoreExitCode turns a non-zero exit into a warning. That warning stays a warning even
    though warnings are errors repo-wide, because TreatWarningsAsErrors is passed to the compiler and
    does not promote MSBuild's own. A peer prints its own diagnostic before exiting, so the streams are
    left at their default importance and the warning below does not restate what they already said.

    Anchored to one project rather than left to fire wherever it is imported. A solution build evaluates
    this file once per project, so without the anchor a single `dotnet build` would repeat every check
    for every project. ihcclient is the anchor because every buildable path here builds it, and because
    the checks read sources, never build output — they do not care whether the other projects have
    compiled yet.
  -->
  <Target Name="StaticCheck"
          AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(RunStaticChecks)' != 'false' AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'ihcclient'">

    <Exec Condition="'$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT'"
          Command="pwsh -NoProfile -File &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)scripts/static_check.ps1&quot;"
          WorkingDirectory="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)"
          IgnoreExitCode="true" ContinueOnError="true">
      <Output TaskParameter="ExitCode" PropertyName="StaticCheckExitCode" />
    </Exec>

    <Exec Condition="'$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT'"
          Command="bash &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)scripts/static_check.sh&quot;"
          WorkingDirectory="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)"
          IgnoreExitCode="true" ContinueOnError="true">
      <Output TaskParameter="ExitCode" PropertyName="StaticCheckExitCode" />
    </Exec>

    <Warning Condition="'$(StaticCheckExitCode)' != '0'"
             Text="A static check could not run (scripts/static_check exited with code $(StaticCheckExitCode)); see the message above it. Build output is unaffected." />
  </Target>

</Project>
